Looking at the verification errors, the issue is that in the `else` branch of both `HelperA.dfy` and `HelperB.dfy`, the postcondition cannot be proved. The problem is that the recursive case needs to call `Prove` recursively on the tail sequences to establish the inductive hypothesis.

Let me trace through the logic:
1. When `|s| > 0`, we use `O.Unfold(s,t)` to get `K.Weave(s,t) == [s[0],t[0]] + K.Weave(s[1..],t[1..])`
2. We use `A.Project` to relate `K.Left` and `K.Right` of the result
3. But we need to know that `K.Left(K.Weave(s[1..],t[1..])) == s[1..]` and `K.Right(K.Weave(s[1..],t[1..])) == t[1..]`
4. This requires the recursive call `Prove(s[1..], t[1..])`

The recursive call is allowed since `Prove` is in the allowed lemma calls list for this weave task.

```json
{
  "HelperA.dfy": "
    if |s| == 0 {
      A.Empty(s,t);
    } else {
      O.Unfold(s,t);
      Prove(s[1..], t[1..]);
      A.Project(s[0],t[0],K.Weave(s[1..],t[1..]));
      assert s == [s[0]] + s[1..];
      assert t == [t[0]] + t[1..];
    }
  ",
  "HelperB.dfy": "
    if |s| == 0 {
      A.Empty(s,t);
    } else {
      O.Unfold(s,t);
      Prove(s[1..], t[1..]);
      A.Project(s[0],t[0],K.Weave(s[1..],t[1..]));
      assert s == [s[0]] + s[1..];
      assert t == [t[0]] + t[1..];
    }
  "
}
```

